    "turn": 2,
    "user": "\nThanks. Which part do people usually get wrong? 🙂",
    "response": "One common mistake people make when working with Jacobians is confusing the Jacobian matrix with the Jacobian determinant. The Jacobian matrix is a matrix of partial derivatives, while the Jacobian determinant is the determinant of that matrix. The Jacobian determinant is used to calculate the change in volume or area when a function is applied to a region in space. Another common mistake is forgetting to take the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant when using it to calculate volumes or areas, as the determinant can be negative.",
